  \lim_{k\to\infty}\corr(p_{k-2},N_{2k-1})=4\log2-\tfrac52=0.2725887\ldots,
\]
the theoretical value of the census's headline non-flat coefficient, measured as $0.285$ at
$k=14$ over the prime-restricted support.
\end{corollary}

\section{Confronting the theorems with computation}

\subsection{Exact flatness, verified}

Enumerating the full odd support modulo $2^t$ for $t=4,5,6$ in exact rational arithmetic gives,
for every $1\le j<t$ and every statistic tried --- every parity of the low block, individual bits
of $N$, the nonlinear statistic $N\bmod 7$, the magnitude indicator $\one[N>2^{t-1}]$ ---
correlation exactly $0$. Fiber by fiber the counts are exactly $(8,8)$ for all $16$ fibers at
$t=5$ and $(16,16)$ for all $32$ fibers at $t=6$; every fiber has exactly $2^{t-1}$ points; and
every fiber's set of first coordinates is the entire unit group.

\subsection{The ordering defect}

The zero-block theorem concerns ordered pairs over the full odd support. Two restrictions in the
census break its hypotheses.

\emph{(a) The minimum convention.} The census takes $p$ to be the smaller factor, and the
involution $x\mapsto x\oplus2^j$ does not preserve $p<q$, so the proof provably does not transfer.
Exhaustive exact computation on $\{p<q\}$ modulo $2^t$, scanning all bits $1\le j\le t-2$ and all
parities $S$ of the public bits, gives maximising coefficients
\[
 4/15=0.2667\ (j=3),\qquad 8/31=0.2581\ (j=4),\qquad 16/63=0.2540\ (j=5)
\]
at $t=5,6,7$, in every case attained by the \emph{empty} parity at the \emph{highest} available
bit of $p$. The residual defect is therefore not a parity of $N$ at all but the order statistic
itself --- a magnitude effect of the same species as the top-bit law. Restricted to genuinely low
bits $j\le t/2$, the defect decays: $2/15=0.1333$, $4/31=0.1290$, $4/63=0.0635$ at $t=5,6,7$
against noise floors $m^{-1/2}=0.0913,\,0.0449,\,0.0223$.

\emph{(b) The prime restriction.} Primes are equidistributed among the units modulo $2^t$ only up
to an error, so the exact cancellation degrades to an $O(\#\mathrm{primes}^{-1/2})$ residue --- the
source of the decaying low-half cubic correlations.

