\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Restricted Walsh spectrum by fast Walsh--Hadamard transform.} Given $f$ as a table on
$\{0,1\}^n$, the in-place butterfly (for each bit $b$, replace each pair $(u,v)$ differing only at
$b$ by $(u+v,u-v)$) computes all $2^n$ coefficients in $O(n2^n)$ operations instead of $O(4^n)$.
For a sparse support one first accumulates the sign-valued function into a dense array indexed by
the public value; a degree-$\le d$ scan then reads off the $\sum_{i\le d}\binom{n}{i}$ coefficients
of small Hamming weight.

\paragraph{Exact fiber-balance certification.} Bucket all $4^{t-1}$ ordered odd pairs by
$pq\bmod2^t$ and, per bucket and per bit $j$, count first coordinates with $p_j=0$ and $p_j=1$;
the theorem predicts $(2^{t-2},2^{t-2})$ in every cell. Cost $O(4^t t)$ in exact integer
arithmetic: a certificate, not an estimate.

\paragraph{Exact top-bit covariance.} Enumerate $\mathcal{B}_k$ and accumulate
$(|A|,|B|,|A\cap B|,|\mathcal{B}_k|)$ as integers; cost $O(4^k)$, reducible to $O(2^k)$ by
replacing the inner loop with the closed form
$\#\{q\in[p,2^k): q\ge 2^{2k-1}/p\}$.

\paragraph{Null calibration.} The maximum of $|\mathcal{F}|$ near-independent standardised
coefficients concentrates near $\sqrt{2\log|\mathcal{F}|}$ in units of $m^{-1/2}$. At $k=14$
($n=28$, $m=380628$) this predicts an all-parity maximum of $\approx6.2\,m^{-1/2}=0.0101$ ---
exactly the reported noise level, which is why an observed maximum of $0.021$ must be read as
noise.

\section{Discussion}

The zero-block theorem is unconditional, exact, and quantifies over all predictors, but it lives on
the odd support modulo $2^t$ with ordered pairs, not on the prime-restricted, minimum-factor
support of a real attack scenario. Both departures are quantified above: the ordered-pair defect is
a magnitude statistic decaying with $t$, and the prime restriction contributes at the
equidistribution scale $\pi(2^k)^{-1/2}$. The top-bit law is unconditional and holds for every
balanced pair, prime or not; what it reveals is size, and size is public.

Together the results bracket the truth from both sides: below the transition window the
correlation is exactly zero; at the very top it is strictly positive at every size. The only open
content is the width of the transition window, measured as roughly six bits.

In the language of barriers: no low-degree $\mathrm{GF}(2)$ approximator to any factor bit exists
--- established exactly on the odd support, in the strong form covering all predictors, and
measured to the resolution of the null model on the prime support --- and all structure computable
from $N$ that the spectrum sees is symmetric in the two factors. The Walsh/$\mathrm{GF}(2)$ face
of the framework is closed: what is flat is provably flat, and what is not flat is provably
harmless.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{conjecture}[The ordering defect is exactly a top-bit statistic]
For $t\ge2$, $1\le j<t$, over the support of ordered odd pairs $p<q$ modulo $2^t$, every
correlation of $p_j$ with a statistic of the low block is nonzero but bounded by
$C\cdot2^{-t/2}$, and the entire defect is carried by the event $p<q$: the correlation equals
$\Cov(\one[p<q],g(N))$ times a factor depending only on $j$, up to $O(2^{-t})$.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Degree hierarchy over the prime support]
For the exact $k$-bit prime semiprime support and every fixed degree $d$ there is $k_0(d)$ such
that for all $k\ge k_0(d)$ and all $1\le j\le k-7$, every parity of degree $\le d$ of the bits of
$N$ correlates with $p_j$ by at most $C_d\,\pi(2^k)^{-1/2}\,\mathrm{polylog}$, while for $j>k-7$
the maximum degree-$1$ correlation stays bounded away from $0$.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Sharp constant]
$\mathrm{cov}_k=(2\log2-1)/4+O(2^{-k})$, and more generally the whole profile
$\corr(p_{k-d},N_{2k-1})$, $d=2,3,4,\dots$, is given by explicit areas of regions cut out of
$\mathcal{S}$, quantifying the six-bit transition window exactly.
\end{conjecture}

Two further directions. \emph{Beyond parities:} the zero-block theorem already covers all
predictors on the odd support, so the natural targets are supports where it fails by design ---
the minimum convention, the prime restriction, and unbalanced factor sizes, where the magnitude
family is richer. \emph{Higher moduli:} every step of the argument uses only that the residues
form a group under multiplication, so the theorem holds verbatim modulo any $M$, with the balance
lemma replaced by the requirement that the chosen statistic of $p$ have mean zero on
$(\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times$; determining which base-$b$ digit statistics are mean-zero on the unit
group modulo $b^t$ would extend the flat block to arbitrary bases.

\section{Conclusion}

The factoring function, viewed through the Walsh spectrum of its secret bits, is spectrally flat:
on the low block, exactly and provably so, for every predictor and not merely for low-degree
parities, with perfect secrecy behind the vanishing. The single non-flat structure is the top-bit
magnitude/carry family, which obeys a deterministic one-sided transmission law, has strictly
positive covariance at every size with explicit limit $(2\log2-1)/4$, and is symmetric in the two
factors --- hence reveals the size of the numbers, never their identity. The remaining empirical
curiosities are finite-support fluctuations of that same family, decaying with the size of the
support. The negative result is complete and carries positive content: on this face of the
problem, there is nothing to find but a ruler.

